"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:07,083\nThe prefrontal cortex: the mind\u2019s workbench\n\n3\n00:00:10,083 --> 00:00:13,233\nIn 1989, Shintaro Funahashi,\n\n4\n00:00:13,465 --> 00:00:19,674\nCharles Bruce and Patricia Goldman-Rakic recorded single cells in the prefrontal cortex of monkeys.\n\n5\n00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:22,453\nA spot of light flashed and disappeared.\n\n6\n00:00:22,979 --> 00:00:24,153\nFor a few seconds,\n\n7\n00:00:24,367 --> 00:00:26,497\nthe monkey had to remember where it had been.\n\n8\n00:00:27,017 --> 00:00:29,556\nSome cells kept firing through that wait,\n\n9\n00:00:29,820 --> 00:00:31,683\nholding the location in mind.\n\n10\n00:00:31,853 --> 00:00:36,047\nThis is working memory: holding information in mind while you use it.\n\n11\n00:00:36,541 --> 00:00:39,267\nThe prefrontal cortex works like a workbench.\n\n12\n00:00:39,564 --> 00:00:41,266\nIt keeps a few things active,\n\n13\n00:00:41,314 --> 00:00:42,785\nso you can compare them,\n\n14\n00:00:42,812 --> 00:00:45,314\ncombine them and plan what to do next.\n\n15\n00:00:46,984 --> 00:00:48,562\nThe workbench is small.\n\n16\n00:00:48,881 --> 00:00:51,939\nMost adults can hold about four chunks at once.\n\n17\n00:00:52,270 --> 00:00:54,427\nExperts get around this by chunking.\n\n18\n00:00:54,879 --> 00:01:00,701\nA chess master shown a real game position for five seconds can rebuild it almost perfectly.\n\n19\n00:01:01,203 --> 00:01:03,184\nWith the pieces placed at random,\n\n20\n00:01:03,321 --> 00:01:05,646\nmost of that advantage disappears.\n\n21\n00:01:07,816 --> 00:01:10,819\nThe prefrontal cortex also keeps you on task.\n\n22\n00:01:11,226 --> 00:01:14,816\nTry naming the ink color when the word spells a different color.\n\n23\n00:01:15,293 --> 00:01:21,017\nReading is automatic, so you slow down while the prefrontal cortex holds back the habit.\n\n24\n00:01:22,687 --> 00:01:25,176\nIt is one of the last regions to mature.\n\n25\n00:01:25,693 --> 00:01:27,470\nScans of the same children,\n\n26\n00:01:27,475 --> 00:01:30,630\ntaken every two years from age four to twenty-one,\n\n27\n00:01:30,873 --> 00:01:34,241\nshowed the areas for seeing and touch maturing first.\n\n28\n00:01:34,724 --> 00:01:37,175\nThe prefrontal cortex matured last.\n\n29\n00:01:39,345 --> 00:01:42,451\nIt is also the region most sensitive to stress.\n\n30\n00:01:42,873 --> 00:01:48,060\nEven mild stress that feels out of your control can quickly weaken working memory.\n\n31\n00:01:49,230 --> 00:01:50,911\nWhy does this matter for learning?\n\n32\n00:01:51,228 --> 00:01:52,898\nDon't overload the workbench.\n\n33\n00:01:53,191 --> 00:01:55,792\nWrite down what you don't need to hold in your head.\n\n34\n00:01:56,164 --> 00:01:58,356\nBreak new material into small steps,\n\n35\n00:01:58,376 --> 00:02:01,221\nand practise until the pieces become chunks.\n\n36\n00:02:01,586 --> 00:02:04,049\nEach chunk frees space for new thinking.\n\n37\n00:02:05,219 --> 00:02:08,171\nThe prefrontal cortex is the mind's workbench.\n\n38\n00:02:08,493 --> 00:02:10,286\nIt holds what you are thinking about,\n\n39\n00:02:10,291 --> 00:02:11,866\nand keeps you on task.\n\n"
